 {'model': 'deepseek-coder-6.7b-instruct', 'response': '
Yes, you can use the same technique on `IEnumerable<T>`. You'll need to convert your `IEnumerable<T>` to `IQueryable<T>` first. Here is a small utility method that can do this:

```csharp
public static IQueryable<T> ToQueryable<T>(this IEnumerable<T> source)
{
    return source.AsQueryable();
}
```

Then, you can use the `OrderBy` method on the `IQueryable<T>`:

```csharp
IEnumerable<Person> people =...
var orderedPeople = people.ToQueryable().OrderBy("Name, Age DESC");
```

This way, you're still taking advantage of the LINQ to SQL integration, but working with `IEnumerable<T>` instead of `IQueryable<T>`.

Keep in mind that this will not make a substantial difference in performance for `IEnumerable<T>` because the query will be executed in memory, unlike with `IQueryable<T>` where the query will be translated into SQL.

Also, please note that using `OrderBy` with a string is a bit of a misuse. You should really be using a lambda expression, like so:

```csharp
var orderedPeople = people.OrderBy(p => p.Name).ThenByDescending(p => p.Age);
```

This way, you're not relying on the string, and you have the benefit of the type safety and compile-time checking.

In general, using `IQueryable` is a good practice when you're working with LINQ, as it allows the query to be translated into SQL (or another form of query) at runtime, depending on the data source. It's a more flexible approach than using `IEnumerable`, as it allows for a wide range of operations that aren't possible with `IEnumerable`.
'}
 {'model': 'deepseek-coder-33b-instruct', 'response': '
Yes, you can achieve similar functionality with `IEnumerable<T>` by using `System.Linq.Dynamic.Core`. 

First of all, make sure that you have installed the `System.Linq.Dynamic.Core` library via NuGet.

Then, you can use the `OrderBy`, `Where`, and other similar methods directly on `IEnumerable<T>`. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Linq.Dynamic.Core;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Assume we have a list of people
        List<Person> people = new List<Person>
        {
            new Person { Name = "John", Age = 25 },
            new Person { Name = "Jane", Age = 30 },
            new Person { Name = "Bob", Age = 40 }
        };

        // Now let's sort them by name first and then by age descending
        var sortedPeople = people.AsQueryable().OrderBy("Name, Age DESC").ToList();

        foreach (var person in sortedPeople)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{person.Name}, {person.Age}");
        }
    }
}

public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}
```

This will output:

```
Bob, 40
Jane, 30
John, 25
```

Please note that `System.Linq.Dynamic.Core` provides more functionalities than just `OrderBy`, such as `Select`, `GroupBy`, `Join`, etc.
'}]

