Yes, you can achieve similar functionality with `IEnumerable<T>` by using `System.Linq.Dynamic.Core`. 

First of all, make sure that you have installed the `System.Linq.Dynamic.Core` library via NuGet.

Then, you can use the `OrderBy`, `Where`, and other similar methods directly on `IEnumerable<T>`. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Linq.Dynamic.Core;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        // Assume we have a list of people
        List<Person> people = new List<Person>
        {
            new Person { Name = "John", Age = 25 },
            new Person { Name = "Jane", Age = 30 },
            new Person { Name = "Bob", Age = 40 }
        };

        // Now let's sort them by name first and then by age descending
        var sortedPeople = people.AsQueryable().OrderBy("Name, Age DESC").ToList();

        foreach (var person in sortedPeople)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{person.Name}, {person.Age}");
        }
    }
}

public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public int Age { get; set; }
}
```

This will output:

```
Bob, 40
Jane, 30
John, 25
```

Please note that `System.Linq.Dynamic.Core` provides more functionalities than just `OrderBy`, such as `Select`, `GroupBy`, `Join`, etc.

