[{'model': 'codellama-13b-instruct', 'response': '  Here is an example of a thread-safe Singleton class in Java using double-checked locking:
```java
public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;
    private static final Object lock = new Object();

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (lock) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }

    public void updateSharedResource(String value) {
        synchronized (lock) {
            // Update the shared resource
        }
    }
}
```
Explanation:

* The `getInstance()` method uses double-checked locking to ensure that the singleton instance is only created once, even in a multithreaded environment.
* The `updateSharedResource()` method is synchronized to ensure that only one thread can update the shared resource at a time.
* The `lock` object is used to synchronize access to the singleton instance and the shared resource.

Here's an example of how to use the `Singleton` class:
```java
Singleton singleton = Singleton.getInstance();
singleton.updateSharedResource("Hello, world!");
```
This code will create a single instance of the `Singleton` class, and then update the shared resource using the `updateSharedResource()` method. The `updateSharedResource()` method is synchronized, so only one thread can update the shared resource at a time.

It's worth noting that this implementation of the Singleton pattern is not thread-safe, as the `getInstance()` method is not synchronized. If multiple threads call `getInstance()` at the same time, they may both create a new instance of the `Singleton` class. To make the `getInstance()` method thread-safe, it should be synchronized as well.
```java
public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
    if (instance == null) {
        instance = new Singleton();
    }
